WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.634 --> 00:00:17.368
As strange as the 
creatures of the microcosmos are,

00:00:17.368 --> 00:00:22.370
their lives still revolve around the
same fundamentals that ours do.

00:00:22.370 --> 00:00:26.620
There’s food, there's reproduction, 
and there's death.

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:30.740
Yes, even microbes, hardy as they can be,
experience death.

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:34.500
In some ways, you could say they invented it.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:40.498
And on our journey through the microcosmos,
we’ve watched those deaths through many lenses.

00:00:40.498 --> 00:00:45.280
Some are slow, calm affairs, while others
are explosive or creepy.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:49.239
And today, we’re going to try 
something new for our channel.

00:00:49.239 --> 00:00:56.149
We have gathered a few of our favorite episodes
about death in the microcosmos so that we

00:00:56.149 --> 00:01:00.140
can see where our journey has taken us.

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:05.732
So yeah, this is the kind of video you can
turn on, and leave on for awhile.

00:01:05.732 --> 00:01:10.196
This first video is also one of our oldest,
 so you’ll notice that a lot of

00:01:10.196 --> 00:01:15.130
footage in it looks very different from what
we show these days because thanks to the support

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:18.299
of our viewers, we’ve been 
able to upgrade our microscope

00:01:18.299 --> 00:01:21.008
multiple times over the 
course of this show.

00:01:21.008 --> 00:01:23.439
So the microscope may be different.

00:01:23.439 --> 00:01:26.963
But the death, well, the death remains the
same.

00:01:30.899 --> 00:01:39.259
This round little unicellular creature came
to us via a plankton net, a mesh with tiny,

00:01:39.259 --> 00:01:45.799
microscopic holes through which we ran hundreds
and hundreds of liters of water, letting us

00:01:45.799 --> 00:01:50.049
collect anything too large to pass through.

00:01:50.049 --> 00:01:54.730
We haven’t been able to identify this species
yet, making it a bit of a mystery.

00:01:54.730 --> 00:02:01.100
But the bigger mystery is still to come because
this little creature is about to undergo that

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:06.326
most universal and unknowable 
experience of all, death.

00:02:09.294 --> 00:02:13.220
Death comes to the microcosmos in many forms.

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:19.260
Like this Stentor Polymorphous, slowly expelling
the contents of its once trumpet-like body

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:21.690
into the surrounding environment.

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:29.176
Or this dead larva, whose exoskeleton is now
an inanimate host to two unicellular organisms.

00:02:29.176 --> 00:02:36.130
Even the mighty tardigrade, which has survived
as a species through multiple mass extinctions,

00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:37.273
is not immune to death.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:46.860
This is, of course, the natural order of things.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:55.920
Predators hunt, and their prey attempts to
survive, with varying levels of success.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:04.120
This is Loxophyllum meleagris, a large unicellular
organism that we’ve shown before eating a rotifer.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:12.780
This one is practically stuffed with those multicellular 
creatures, we counted five rotifers inside of it.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:18.740
But sometimes the predator becomes the prey,
and even the Loxophyllum meleagris has to

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:25.010
find ways to ensure its survival when other
species come after it.

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:29.400
This seemingly unlikely threat is the Lacrymaria
olor.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:37.210
Its name in Latin means “tears of a swan”,
a name that suits both its teardrop shape

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:45.720
and its neck-like extension, which gets up to 
eight times longer than its body in search of prey.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:52.130
Sometimes, we can see its neck poking out
of the dirt on our microscope slide.

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:57.290
But even knowing that, you’d be forgiven
for thinking it unlikely that something so

00:03:57.290 --> 00:04:02.040
small could pose a problem for those larger
Loxophyllum.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:10.300
And yet, the Lacrymaria manages to take quite
a chunk out of the Loxophyllum.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:17.000
The Loxophyllum though, survives thanks to its 
ability to regenerate the piece that was taken,

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:20.400
but not all prey gets so lucky.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:28.650
Here, this rotifer has been killed by a heliozoan,
destined to become food, a fate that this

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:33.680
flagellate is about to share as it becomes
captured by a heliozoan that is in the middle

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:35.370
of cell division.

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:41.740
The flagellate has been trapped by those long
extensions, called axopods, that radiate out

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:43.570
from the heliozoan’s body.

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:49.890
As the flagellate comes further in, it will
be engulfed by the cells into its own food

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:52.270
compartment called a vacuole.

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:58.880
There, it will be lysed open and its contents
digested by the heliozoa.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:02.620
In the end though, the natural order comes
for predators too.

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:07.455
Here, another heliozoan’s dying cellular
body attracts the various

00:05:07.455 --> 00:05:14.060
decomposers of the microbial world.

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:20.340
Aside from predators, there are many other
factors that lead a single-celled organism

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:28.520
to die, changes in temperature, oxygen 
concentration, pH, water quality, so much more.

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:34.400
This single-celled organism is swollen because
the water surrounding it is entering the cell

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:36.490
via osmosis.

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:42.920
Many organisms have water pumps called contractile
vacuoles that they use to push water back

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:46.180
out and prevent that swelling.

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:52.280
But as in the case of this organism, sometimes
those contractile vacuoles stop working,

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:57.980
and when that happens, 
the cell swells and explodes.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:06.260
Other times, the cause of death is harder
to determine, like this Paradileptus that

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:13.350
spent several hours swimming before going
still, its shape beginning to change until

00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:20.460
it melts away, seeming to kill not only the
Paradileptus but this small green cell swimming

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:26.087
nearby, but leaving other smaller flagellates
seemingly unaffected.

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:33.710
And this brings us back to the beginning,
with our mystery organism that is about to

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:38.760
undergo a death laden with more mysteries.

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:44.830
At first, the cell looks like it’s just
melting away, dissolving into something that

00:06:44.830 --> 00:06:53.330
resembles a microbial Milky Way, except that
for a few seconds, it almost looks like the

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:57.480
cell membrane is able to close itself back up.

00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:02.780
We think, though we can’t know for sure,
that some of the mechanisms inside the cell

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:07.800
are still working, and that the organism is
trying to recover.

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:12.449
But alas, survival is not in the cards.

00:07:12.449 --> 00:07:18.750
Its membrane goes through lysis, releasing
its insides into the surrounding environment.

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:25.240
This death is unlike any other kind of death
we’ve observed under our microscope, and

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:28.410
we’re still not sure what caused it.

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:33.520
Perhaps there were so many organisms in the
sample that they depleted the oxygen, and

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:37.609
this organism could not continue cellular respiration.

00:07:37.609 --> 00:07:40.798
But perhaps it was something else.

00:07:40.798 --> 00:07:47.330
Death at every size holds its own mysteries,
but it also reveals.

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:52.420
The observations we make, even the guesses
we come up with, tell us about the way these

00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:58.590
microbes interact with their environment,
the way their own bodies work, and the connections

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:01.010
that exist between them.

00:08:01.010 --> 00:08:08.790
It is only ever in the mysteries that knowledge
is waiting to be found.

00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:15.130
So we just saw a small fraction of how many
ways there are for microbes to die.

00:08:15.130 --> 00:08:21.280
But maybe now you’re asking yourself a more
fundamental question: what even is death?

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:26.960
Well, weirdly, none of us will ever 
fully know the answer.

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:33.760
But that doesn’t mean we can’t try to
use what we know of chemistry and life to

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:38.079
begin to describe it, as we’ll see in our
next video.

00:08:40.639 --> 00:08:44.839
This is a ciliate, Loxodes magnus.

00:08:44.839 --> 00:08:48.040
It is about to die.

00:08:49.954 --> 00:08:54.209
Of course, depending on your time scale, 
we’re all about to die.

00:08:54.209 --> 00:09:01.079
To the grand canyon, or the sun, things that
have existed for millions or billions of years,

00:09:01.079 --> 00:09:07.941
we are each weird little bubbles of peculiar
chemistry that form and then pop,

00:09:07.941 --> 00:09:11.290
form, and then pop.

00:09:11.290 --> 00:09:16.329
But this ciliate, and with our new microscope
you can really see those cilia beating, is

00:09:16.329 --> 00:09:19.689
about to pop right before your eyes.

00:09:19.689 --> 00:09:21.999
It looks fine right now.

00:09:21.999 --> 00:09:27.699
You can even see, inside it, it’s last meal,
a Trachelomonas.

00:09:27.699 --> 00:09:30.319
So we don’t think it’s starving to death.

00:09:30.319 --> 00:09:33.100
It seems to be trucking along just fine.

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:39.730
Loxodes Magnus are microaerophilic organisms,
preferring a low concentration of dissolved

00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:43.160
oxygen in their environment, but not too low.

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:49.589
So maybe the concentration on the slide was
too high, though we’ve witnessed many others

00:09:49.589 --> 00:09:53.709
who have been just fine in our preparations.

00:09:53.709 --> 00:10:00.040
So no, we can’t tell you why this ciliate
is about to die, but we can tell you that

00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:08.420
right here, that’s where James, our master
of microscopes, first saw something strange.

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:15.470
The moment the ciliate shifted direction,
a little trail of cell membrane and cytoplasm.

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:17.370
No reason.

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:20.550
Nothing grabbed it, it didn’t snag on anything.

00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:28.610
But a little bit of what was once a part of the 
organism was suddenly, no longer a part of it.

00:10:28.610 --> 00:10:36.240
That cytoplasm is full of complicated molecules
that are what chemists would call, far from equilibrium.

00:10:39.466 --> 00:10:46.589
Equilibrium is the situation in which chemicals
no longer have a tendency to react over time.

00:10:46.589 --> 00:10:53.699
In general, a thing that you can say for sure
is that all the stuff outside of living cells

00:10:53.699 --> 00:10:58.269
is either at chemical equilibrium, or it is
headed there.

00:10:58.269 --> 00:11:05.699
Whereas stuff inside cells is not at equilibrium,
and it’s not headed there either.

00:11:06.968 --> 00:11:14.829
How are all of these chemicals that, if left
alone, would rapidly reach equilibrium managing

00:11:14.829 --> 00:11:16.752
to not do that?

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:18.809
Life.

00:11:19.863 --> 00:11:22.729
That is what life is.

00:11:22.729 --> 00:11:29.747
A bunch of chemicals that take in energy in
order to keep each other from reaching equilibrium.

00:11:35.856 --> 00:11:44.019
Quick break from our friend, the way we define
life in biology classes is, wrong.

00:11:44.019 --> 00:11:48.281
It’s not even really a definition, it’s
a set of qualifying factors.

00:11:48.281 --> 00:11:50.410
Life has to take in energy.

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:57.860
Life has to reproduce, it must respond to
its environment, it must consist of cells.

00:11:57.860 --> 00:12:04.310
This is not a definition, it’s an attempt
to draw a line, to create a boundary.

00:12:04.310 --> 00:12:11.399
And that makes sense for things that are actually
amorphous and complicated, like social constructs.

00:12:11.399 --> 00:12:17.533
But life is not a construct of our opinions,
but of reality.

00:12:17.533 --> 00:12:25.303
Life is a chemical system that uses energy
to keep itself from reaching chemical equilibrium.

00:12:27.087 --> 00:12:28.022
Why do they do it?

00:12:29.743 --> 00:12:33.319
Oh, well maybe let’s not go that deep,
at least not today.

00:12:33.319 --> 00:12:39.749
Suffice it to say, a system that did this
developed on this planet and now, billions

00:12:39.749 --> 00:12:45.629
of years later, it is still doing it.

00:12:45.629 --> 00:12:51.010
We have many things in common with this ciliate,
and not to belabor the point, but one of those

00:12:51.010 --> 00:12:53.850
things is that we will die.

00:12:53.850 --> 00:13:01.230
You’ve may have noticed by now that this
video isn’t about what life is, it’s about

00:13:01.230 --> 00:13:02.769
what death is.

00:13:02.769 --> 00:13:06.839
It’s just that, first, we had to define life.

00:13:06.839 --> 00:13:12.749
Life is chemicals working together to take
in energy to keep themselves far from equilibrium.

00:13:12.749 --> 00:13:17.209
Death is not the return to chemical equilibrium.

00:13:17.209 --> 00:13:20.680
The process of decay can last decades.

00:13:20.680 --> 00:13:27.079
Likewise, many parts of my body will return
to equilibrium over the course of my life,

00:13:27.079 --> 00:13:31.569
I’m shedding skin cells right now and so are you.

00:13:31.569 --> 00:13:38.370
The atoms and molecules of my body are replaced
with new ones over and over and over again.

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:41.939
But I will only die once.

00:13:41.939 --> 00:13:49.350
Likewise, our ciliate has been shedding cytoplasm
and cell membrane for minutes now, and that

00:13:49.350 --> 00:13:53.470
shed cytoplasm is dead, no doubt.

00:13:53.470 --> 00:13:55.949
But the organism lives.

00:13:55.949 --> 00:13:58.699
Its chemistry continues.

00:13:58.699 --> 00:14:01.170
For now.

00:14:01.170 --> 00:14:10.339
Death is the moment when the system that maintains
the far from equilibrium state ceases existence.

00:14:10.339 --> 00:14:13.220
And we can imagine that at many scales.

00:14:13.220 --> 00:14:18.670
That can happen to individual bits of an organism,
as it is happening to the chemicals spilling

00:14:18.670 --> 00:14:21.059
out of our Loxodes right now.

00:14:21.059 --> 00:14:25.009
It can also happen to an individual cell in
an organism.

00:14:25.009 --> 00:14:26.189
And that happens all the time.

00:14:26.189 --> 00:14:30.079
It is happening right now inside you.

00:14:30.079 --> 00:14:32.639
It can also happen to an organism.

00:14:32.639 --> 00:14:38.509
That’s what we usually think of as death,
with our focus, so often, on the individual.

00:14:38.509 --> 00:14:43.579
But we can keep moving up the scale and find
yet other kinds of death.

00:14:43.579 --> 00:14:49.180
When a common genetic system that was useful
for keeping many similar but individual organisms

00:14:49.180 --> 00:14:54.116
alive ceases to exist, that is an extinction.

00:14:54.116 --> 00:14:56.019
A kind of death.

00:14:56.019 --> 00:15:01.670
And when the system that has kept all life
on earth far from equilibrium for billions

00:15:01.670 --> 00:15:08.639
of years, that system that we all share of
nucleic and amino acids, when that ceases

00:15:08.639 --> 00:15:13.190
to exist, that will be something else.

00:15:13.190 --> 00:15:18.739
A terrible kind of death that we do not even
have a name for.

00:15:18.739 --> 00:15:20.569
But it will be a death.

00:15:20.569 --> 00:15:29.344
The largest death, I suppose, until heat death,
when everything in the universe has found equilibrium.

00:15:41.319 --> 00:15:43.860
Our ciliate is about out of time now.

00:15:43.860 --> 00:15:50.049
I don’t know when we can call it, when we
can pronounce the time of death, but this

00:15:50.049 --> 00:15:52.291
seems as good a time as any.

00:15:52.291 --> 00:15:55.243
Here, we have death.

00:15:55.243 --> 00:16:02.237
The system that was using energy to keep itself
from reaching equilibrium has ceased to exist.

00:16:08.045 --> 00:16:11.392
Hey, welcome back. If you’ve come 
out of that video with some

00:16:11.392 --> 00:16:16.389
existential dread about the state of the universe,
that is very reasonable.

00:16:16.389 --> 00:16:21.431
However, on our next stop in this journey,
we’re going to argue that sure, chemical

00:16:21.431 --> 00:16:28.586
equilibria are scary, but if you’re a nematode,
maybe you should worry about fungi first.

00:16:39.986 --> 00:16:44.119
There are plenty of horror stories that begin
innocuously enough.

00:16:44.119 --> 00:16:50.629
A new home, a camping trip with friends, a
doll purchased at an estate sale….

00:16:50.629 --> 00:16:57.339
This one starts with some ponds, the same
set of ponds that James, our master of microscopes,

00:16:57.339 --> 00:17:01.569
has been sampling every week for the past
three years.

00:17:01.569 --> 00:17:05.640
Which means that he’s collected so many
microbes from these waters that you might

00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:08.780
think they’d get a bit boring or redundant.

00:17:08.780 --> 00:17:14.426
But you should never underestimate nature’s
capacity for surprise.

00:17:19.941 --> 00:17:24.620
Recently, James came home with some samples
from these ponds.

00:17:24.620 --> 00:17:30.920
And as usual, he prepared some slides and
checked on the organisms within, finding some

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:34.940
nematodes like this one slithering about on
the slide.

00:17:34.940 --> 00:17:40.889
And all seemed well, so he stored the slides
and his new friends in a humidity chamber

00:17:40.889 --> 00:17:44.019
and waited to observe them after a few more
days.

00:17:44.019 --> 00:17:50.760
But two days later, all would not be well.

00:17:50.760 --> 00:17:53.280
This is where we build our suspense.

00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:59.130
In a movie, this would be the moment where
we assess the unsettling basement or the dark

00:17:59.130 --> 00:18:02.520
woods, and then consider retreating to safety.

00:18:02.520 --> 00:18:07.575
This is the creepy doll, only there hasn’t
been any thumps in the middle of the night,

00:18:10.500 --> 00:18:12.288
so everything seems okay, right?

00:18:16.224 --> 00:18:20.640
We’re looking at the spores of a fungus,
one belonging to the group Arthrobotrys.

00:18:20.640 --> 00:18:26.470
And when it’s just floating around like
this, it seems quite harmless—especially

00:18:26.470 --> 00:18:31.080
when compared to the nematodes we showed earlier,
which are part of a whole family of worms

00:18:31.080 --> 00:18:34.669
that are notorious for their parasitic lifestyle.

00:18:34.669 --> 00:18:41.097
And if you were to write off Arthrobotrys
as a potential threat, you would be correct…

00:18:41.097 --> 00:18:43.084
most of the time.

00:18:43.084 --> 00:18:48.280
It does spend much of its life aligned with
the dead, but only to sustain itself on the

00:18:48.280 --> 00:18:51.940
remains of decayed life and organic matter.

00:18:51.940 --> 00:18:57.490
Arthrobotrys species are found all around
the world, occupying everything from soil

00:18:57.490 --> 00:19:03.310
to animal feces in the many varied climates
that make up our planet.

00:19:03.310 --> 00:19:08.960
And wherever it is, the fungus ensures that
nutrients like nitrogen from dead organisms

00:19:08.960 --> 00:19:12.540
and other waste cycle through ecosystems.

00:19:12.540 --> 00:19:20.560
But when nitrogen is scarce, these fungi will
resort to hunting it down from living sources.

00:19:20.560 --> 00:19:26.269
And what better prey than the nematode, a
fellow dweller of the soil and one of the

00:19:26.269 --> 00:19:30.490
most abundant animals on earth?

00:19:30.490 --> 00:19:37.130
When James put his slides into the humidity
chamber, he had no notion of what these nematodes

00:19:37.130 --> 00:19:43.440
would be facing, and so no expectation of
what he would find.

00:19:43.440 --> 00:19:49.760
But when the slides came back out, what he
observed was something he’d only seen once

00:19:49.760 --> 00:19:57.360
before, in a drawing done two years ago by
one of his close friends, Katelyn Solbakk.

00:19:57.360 --> 00:20:01.770
In it, you can see a nematode 
whose body has been clinched

00:20:01.770 --> 00:20:05.330
into segments by some kind of bulbous, thing.

00:20:05.330 --> 00:20:12.320
What you’re seeing is the fungus’ most
brutal design.

00:20:12.320 --> 00:20:19.830
But to get there, it must morph from decomposer
to predator, no longer consuming what has

00:20:19.830 --> 00:20:24.000
already been dead, but actively killing.

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:28.169
It begins by weaving a trap out of itself.

00:20:28.169 --> 00:20:35.610
It threads the hyphae of its mycelium out
and then back in, forming a living loop that

00:20:35.610 --> 00:20:38.860
repeats to form a net.

00:20:38.860 --> 00:20:43.960
But a net is only one part of a trap, the
other part is the lure.

00:20:43.960 --> 00:20:50.350
The fungi can find nematodes by following
traces of their pheromones like they’re

00:20:50.350 --> 00:20:51.350
breadcrumbs.

00:20:51.350 --> 00:20:58.330
And more nefariously, they can mimic the smell
of certain food cues to draw the worm in,

00:20:58.330 --> 00:21:02.890
like a siren working through scent instead
of song.

00:21:02.890 --> 00:21:09.019
The nematode has no reason to suspect anything,
even as it swims closer and closer and eventually

00:21:09.019 --> 00:21:10.750
through the fungal rings.

00:21:10.750 --> 00:21:17.299
But as it does, the movement of worm and water
triggers the rings to constrict.

00:21:17.299 --> 00:21:25.110
The worm is trapped, but the worst is still
yet to come.

00:21:25.110 --> 00:21:33.740
The fungus’ hyphae begin to grow off from
the loop, puncturing the worm’s cuticle and paralyzing it.

00:21:33.740 --> 00:21:41.740
The threads swell up into a bulb that produces
more hyphae to spread through the rest of the nematode.

00:21:41.740 --> 00:21:49.309
And then the fungus feeds and feeds, quickly
digesting the rest of the nematode’s body

00:21:49.309 --> 00:21:51.409
from within.

00:21:51.409 --> 00:21:53.370
It is a gruesome death.

00:21:57.844 --> 00:22:01.679
Here is one nematode, just recently trapped.

00:22:01.679 --> 00:22:05.720
And here is the worm again, four days later.

00:22:05.720 --> 00:22:11.570
You can see the infection bulb where the fungus
first punctured and expanded.

00:22:11.570 --> 00:22:18.240
And the whole body of the worm seems taken
over, no longer a clear tube, but instead

00:22:18.240 --> 00:22:22.527
a corpse that has become home to its cause
of death.

00:22:27.840 --> 00:22:33.610
The Arthrobotrys fungi are not the only ones
capable of trapping and feeding upon nematodes.

00:22:33.610 --> 00:22:39.549
There is a whole range of nematode-trapping
fungi with their own methods, though the species

00:22:39.549 --> 00:22:46.789
Arthrobotrys oligospora is perhaps the most
plentiful of these fungi and also the best studied.

00:22:46.789 --> 00:22:53.019
Maybe it’s just us, but it’s somewhat
unsettling to realize that this insidiousness

00:22:53.019 --> 00:23:00.970
is all the work of a fungus, a thing that
can seem so inert compared to the wiggling,

00:23:00.970 --> 00:23:03.740
active worm that it targets.

00:23:03.740 --> 00:23:07.700
But fungi do have a kinship with horror stories.

00:23:07.700 --> 00:23:12.720
Their frequent role as decomposers naturally
connects them with the dead.

00:23:12.720 --> 00:23:20.690
Plus, they come equipped with their own creeping
sense of dread with images of mycelia weaving through bodies.

00:23:20.690 --> 00:23:24.760
And authors have drawn inspiration from the
notion of fungal horror.

00:23:24.760 --> 00:23:30.769
There are many works--like the famous Gothic
tale We Have Always Lived in the Castle, or

00:23:30.769 --> 00:23:37.289
the short story “The Voice in the Night,”
or recent novels like Mexican Gothic and Wanderers—

00:23:37.289 --> 00:23:44.470
that draw on everything from poisonous mushrooms
to colonizing fungi to create their terror.

00:23:44.470 --> 00:23:51.750
But whatever we seek to scare ourselves with
in fiction, horror has its purpose in nature.

00:23:51.750 --> 00:23:56.529
As we’ve pointed out, nematodes are one
of the most abundant animals on earth.

00:23:56.529 --> 00:24:02.350
They play an important role in decomposition...but
they’re also the source of many diseases—both

00:24:02.350 --> 00:24:04.840
in animal bodies and in plants.

00:24:04.840 --> 00:24:09.620
So having them be slightly less abundant is
important to our ecosystem as well.

00:24:09.620 --> 00:24:15.320
In fact, scientists have been studying these
fungi to develop better nematode-fighting

00:24:15.320 --> 00:24:18.090
strategies for agriculture.

00:24:18.090 --> 00:24:24.039
So as is the case with many good horror villains,
there is a version of this story where the

00:24:24.039 --> 00:24:27.740
nematode-trapping fungus is the hero.

00:24:27.740 --> 00:24:31.818
Unless, of course, you’re the nematode.

00:24:37.669 --> 00:24:42.640
And for our last video, our microbes are dying
at the hands of an unusual enemy.

00:24:42.640 --> 00:24:47.252
It’s James, with an UV laser, in the laboratory.

00:24:47.252 --> 00:24:52.809
Maybe it sounds like a microscopic version of
the game Clue, but there’s a point to it all,

00:24:52.809 --> 00:24:54.877
we swear.

00:24:58.963 --> 00:25:02.570
Blepharisma have appeared on our channel several
times before.

00:25:02.570 --> 00:25:08.889
In fact, this channel got its start thanks
to a video that James, our master of microscopes,

00:25:08.889 --> 00:25:11.779
once posted of a Blepharisma dying.

00:25:11.779 --> 00:25:16.889
Around three million people watched that video,
including me, your host Hank Green.

00:25:16.889 --> 00:25:21.130
So if you enjoy this channel, you can thank
that dead Blepharisma.

00:25:21.130 --> 00:25:24.210
But perhaps you should wait for another day
to thank them.

00:25:24.210 --> 00:25:29.080
Because in about ten seconds, you’re going
to watch a Blepharisma explode.

00:25:29.080 --> 00:25:33.179
Here it is, glowing with autofluorescence
underneath UV light.

00:25:33.179 --> 00:25:39.320
You can see its oblong shape and oral groove
outlined in red…but not for long.

00:25:39.320 --> 00:25:46.039
The red becomes brighter and brighter, but
it also looks like it’s starting to expand.

00:25:46.039 --> 00:25:55.080
And then suddenly, the walls of the blepharisma
burst, the organism popping like a crimson balloon.

00:25:55.080 --> 00:25:58.881
The blepharisma bubbles and pours into its
surroundings

00:25:58.881 --> 00:26:02.420
and it all happens within a matter of seconds.

00:26:02.420 --> 00:26:04.660
Let’s watch it again.

00:26:21.110 --> 00:26:25.659
Dead or dying microbes are a common enough
sight in our journey through the microcosmos.

00:26:25.659 --> 00:26:31.988
And there are many potential culprits behind
these deaths: predators, accidents, environmental

00:26:31.988 --> 00:26:36.000
changes, the inevitable march of life into
death.

00:26:36.630 --> 00:26:41.280
But the culprit this time… well, it was
us.

00:26:41.280 --> 00:26:45.820
Us and the UV light that is part of our new
fluorescence microscope upgrade.

00:26:45.820 --> 00:26:49.279
And our UV light has been very exciting for
us.

00:26:49.279 --> 00:26:54.220
In particular, it’s allowed us to look for
methanogens, or Archaea, which sometimes take

00:26:54.220 --> 00:26:56.679
up residence inside protists.

00:26:56.679 --> 00:27:01.669
Under normal light, it’s hard to tell the
tiny archaea and the tiny bacteria apart.

00:27:01.669 --> 00:27:05.090
But under UV light, the archaea will shine
blue.

00:27:05.090 --> 00:27:09.299
So UV can reveal new aspects of the microcosmos.

00:27:09.299 --> 00:27:13.929
But if you’ve ever fallen asleep on a beach
or just stayed out in the sun a bit too long,

00:27:13.929 --> 00:27:20.700
you may have also experienced the darker side
of UV light.

00:27:20.700 --> 00:27:26.640
No one wants a sunburn, but fortunately, we
have defenses, like hair, and melanin, and

00:27:26.640 --> 00:27:32.860
sunscreen which can block or absorb UV rays
before they cause further damage in our cells.

00:27:32.860 --> 00:27:38.559
We also, and this is crucial, have more than
one cell...so if some of them die, which when

00:27:38.559 --> 00:27:42.779
you get a sunburn they do, the rest of our
bodies can live on.

00:27:42.779 --> 00:27:45.620
Not all organisms have these sorts of protections.

00:27:45.620 --> 00:27:52.624
Or if they do, they’re designed for exposure
to the sun, not the intense scrutiny of our UV light.

00:28:04.389 --> 00:28:08.170
So when James wants to hunt Archaea, he has
to be careful.

00:28:08.170 --> 00:28:11.570
He can quickly shine the UV light to see if
anything blue appears.

00:28:11.570 --> 00:28:14.399
But he has to quickly shut it off.

00:28:14.399 --> 00:28:20.129
Because as we’ve seen, even a few seconds
of exposure to the UV light will kill off

00:28:20.129 --> 00:28:21.909
his pond buddies.

00:28:21.909 --> 00:28:28.019
We want to note that as we said earlier, death
is a common reality of the microcosmos…we

00:28:28.019 --> 00:28:33.500
just usually prefer to walk in on a microbe
dying rather than being the cause of death.

00:28:33.500 --> 00:28:39.110
But for this episode, we decided to make an
exception and use our UV light for an extended

00:28:39.110 --> 00:28:45.070
period of time, with the knowledge that it
would kill the microbe we were watching.

00:28:45.070 --> 00:28:50.455
Because these explosions illustrate the cost
of doing business with light.

00:28:52.950 --> 00:28:56.740
The word for this business is phototoxicity.

00:28:56.740 --> 00:28:58.530
Death by light.

00:28:58.530 --> 00:29:03.659
And while it can happen under other monochromatic
lights, the particular wavelength and intensity

00:29:03.659 --> 00:29:10.039
of our UV light makes it much more harmful
to our organisms than our other red, blue,

00:29:10.039 --> 00:29:12.129
or green light sources.

00:29:12.129 --> 00:29:15.519
This death starts with excitation.

00:29:15.519 --> 00:29:21.679
When the light hits the organism, it can potentially
excite chemical structures inside the cell,

00:29:21.679 --> 00:29:26.879
sending electrons up and down, and producing
fluorescent colors in the process.

00:29:26.879 --> 00:29:30.830
But the colors aren’t the only 
thing that gets created.

00:29:30.830 --> 00:29:35.710
If there’s oxygen around, it will react
with the excited fluorescent molecule, creating

00:29:35.710 --> 00:29:39.899
what are known as reactive oxygen species.

00:29:39.899 --> 00:29:46.460
In biology, reactive oxygen species are byproducts
of different cellular processes that metabolize

00:29:46.460 --> 00:29:50.080
oxygen, which can make them part of normal
life.

00:29:50.080 --> 00:29:54.580
There are even reactive oxygen species that
are involved in signaling pathways.

00:29:54.580 --> 00:30:02.240
But the “reactive” in their name is key
to what makes an excess amount of them dangerous.

00:30:02.240 --> 00:30:07.830
If you are an organism, and you are, there
are a lot of reactions you want to have happen

00:30:07.830 --> 00:30:09.490
in your cells.

00:30:09.490 --> 00:30:15.570
You want your DNA to link together correctly,
you want your enzymes to find the right substrates.

00:30:15.570 --> 00:30:21.539
But reactive oxygen species are happy to react
with all of those molecules too, damaging

00:30:21.539 --> 00:30:27.159
them and getting in the way of the chemistry
that we need to survive.

00:30:27.159 --> 00:30:33.050
What phototoxicity will look like depends
on the organism and the light being directed at it.

00:30:39.019 --> 00:30:43.779
For the organisms we’ve been showing here,
like this homalozoon, the overall effect of

00:30:43.779 --> 00:30:51.450
this intense UV light seems to be unanimous:
the cell swells up and bursts open, like a

00:30:51.450 --> 00:30:54.860
galaxy erupting on our slide.

00:30:54.860 --> 00:30:59.710
But while the overall effect is the same,
the internal machinations are likely different,

00:30:59.710 --> 00:31:05.370
triggered by a complex interplay of different
chemicals that nonetheless react to our light

00:31:05.370 --> 00:31:10.119
source in a similar, catastrophic fashion.

00:31:10.119 --> 00:31:14.740
While we’re not sure of the culprits behind
the homalozoon’s death, we can identify

00:31:14.740 --> 00:31:18.940
one of the chemicals that likely sets off
the blepharisma’s death.

00:31:18.940 --> 00:31:24.129
It’s the reddish pigment molecule called
blepharismin that gives the ciliate its color

00:31:24.129 --> 00:31:27.009
under more normal circumstances.

00:31:27.009 --> 00:31:32.669
Outside of the UV light, you can see the membrane-bound
pigments neatly distributed along the rows

00:31:32.669 --> 00:31:36.679
that stretch from one end of the blepharisma
to the other.

00:31:36.679 --> 00:31:42.610
But under our UV light and with oxygen in
the environment, the blepharismin reacts to

00:31:42.610 --> 00:31:49.610
form reactive oxygen species, and death follows
quickly from there.

00:31:49.610 --> 00:31:55.289
But while toxic in our experiment, we should
note that the blepharismin serves a key purpose

00:31:55.289 --> 00:31:58.389
for the blepharisma: defense.

00:31:58.389 --> 00:32:03.879
These pigment molecules are toxic to some
of Blepharisma’s predators in both the light

00:32:03.879 --> 00:32:05.860
and the dark.

00:32:05.860 --> 00:32:13.865
That makes the pigment somewhat like UV light:
necessary for survival, yet also a delicate negotiation.

00:32:14.909 --> 00:32:20.010
But in the same way that we manage our relationship
with the sun, scientists have learned ways

00:32:20.010 --> 00:32:22.600
to manage these phototoxic reactions.

00:32:22.600 --> 00:32:27.879
They’ve had to in order to understand how
we can use fluorescence microscopy to study

00:32:27.879 --> 00:32:29.649
cells and organisms.

00:32:29.649 --> 00:32:35.230
They’ve learned how to modulate wavelength
and intensity and duration, along with many

00:32:35.230 --> 00:32:39.559
other factors, to wield light in a way that
better serves their purposes.

00:32:39.559 --> 00:32:44.559
In the case of the blepharisma, for example,
scientists found that using a moderate light

00:32:44.559 --> 00:32:48.379
for around 1 hour wasn’t much of a problem
for them.

00:32:48.379 --> 00:32:52.789
But with more time under the light, the cells
would eventually die.

00:32:52.789 --> 00:32:58.990
It’s easy to think of the microcosmos as
a separate world from us, even when we know

00:32:58.990 --> 00:33:02.999
that the microscope is a bridge between large
and small.

00:33:02.999 --> 00:33:09.230
But these deaths at the hand of our supposed
bridge are a cautionary sign that we are encountering

00:33:09.230 --> 00:33:15.279
microbes in a world that is both natural and
manufactured at the same time.

00:33:15.279 --> 00:33:20.700
The way that we light that world impacts the
way we see the organisms, and it also shapes

00:33:20.700 --> 00:33:29.232
their lives—reminding us that they are stronger
often than we can fathom, but fragile nonetheless.

00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:41.686
And that brings us to the end of our tour of death
in the microcosmos today,

00:33:41.686 --> 00:33:46.120
an end to a story of ends, you might say.

00:33:46.120 --> 00:33:51.399
But maybe what we’ve seen today is that
there really is no end, is there?

00:33:51.399 --> 00:33:59.598
Just pauses on individual stories that nonetheless
endure in the remains of the world left behind.

00:33:59.598 --> 00:34:05.608
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:34:05.608 --> 00:34:13.740
And thank you to all of our patrons who make
videos like the ones we’ve watched today possible.

00:34:13.740 --> 00:34:18.600
This channel could not exist without your
support and we are so thankful for it.

00:34:18.600 --> 00:34:23.480
If you’d like to join the list of patrons
you’re currently seeing on your screen,

00:34:23.480 --> 00:34:28.040
you can go to patreon.com/journeytomicro.

00:34:28.040 --> 00:34:31.310
And if you’d like to see more from our Master
of Microscopes, James, you can check

00:34:31.310 --> 00:34:34.400
out Jam &amp; Germs on Instagram, and if you’d
like to see more from us, there’s probably

00:34:34.400 --> 00:34:36.730
a subscribe button somewhere nearby.

